---
import '@fontsource/della-respira';
import '@fontsource/josefin-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/josefin-sans/500.css';
import '../../styles/global.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Emilie Flöge: Movement Through Fabric — a Salon Format learning experience case study." />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href={`${base}brand/salon-format-mark.png`} />
    <title>Emilie Flöge: Movement Through Fabric — Salon Format</title>
  </head>
  <body class="case-page emilie-case">
    <header class="case-header">
      <a href={base} aria-label="Back to Salon Format"><img src={`${base}brand/salon-format-mark.png`} alt="" /></a>
      <a href={`${base}#projects`}>All projects</a>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="case-hero">
        <div class="case-hero-copy">
          <p class="project-status">Work in progress · Working prototype</p>
          <h1>Emilie Flöge:<br /><em>Movement Through Fabric</em></h1>
          <p class="case-deck">An immersive digital museum experience about fashion, movement and creative independence.</p>
          <div class="case-actions">
            <a class="case-button" href="https://emilie-bewegung-aus-stoff.isabella-kohout.chatgpt.site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Experience the prototype <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="case-hero-art" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span>
          <img src={`${base}images/emilie/figure.png`} alt="" />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-intro">
        <p class="case-number">The design question</p>
        <h2>How can a visitor understand historical design through action—not only through reading?</h2>
        <p>The prototype invites visitors to experience how cut, material and weight influence a body’s freedom of movement. Emilie Flöge emerges not as a supporting figure, but as a fashion designer and independent entrepreneur.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-process dark-section">
        <div class="process-heading">
          <p class="case-number">Learning approach</p>
          <h2>Act first.<br />Then understand.</h2>
        </div>
        <ol class="journey-list">
          <li><span>Restriction</span><p>Begin with a body held tightly by fabric.</p></li>
          <li><span>Release</span><p>Pull a drawstring and make space visible.</p></li>
          <li><span>Context</span><p>Connect the physical change to Vienna around 1900.</p></li>
          <li><span>Salon</span><p>Explore material, cut and business inside Schwestern Flöge.</p></li>
          <li><span>Studio</span><p>Experiment with width, weight and layers.</p></li>
          <li><span>Insight</span><p>Recognise movement as a design decision.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="case-image-section">
        <figure class="historical-figure">
          <img src={`${base}images/emilie/historical-photo.jpg`} alt="Emilie Flöge and Gustav Klimt at Lake Attersee in 1909" />
          <figcaption>Historical source material becomes context—not decoration. Photograph: Wien Museum, CC0.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="case-image-copy">
          <p class="case-number">From archive to interaction</p>
          <h2>A museum experience organised around movement.</h2>
          <p>The experience moves between historical evidence, spatial exploration and direct manipulation. Each interaction has a learning role: it reveals a relationship that would be harder to understand through exposition alone.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="decision-section">
        <p class="case-number">Key design decisions</p>
        <div class="decision-grid">
          <article><h3>Embodied entry</h3><p>The first meaningful action happens on the garment itself, making freedom of movement concrete.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Spatial discovery</h3><p>Three traces in the salon reveal material, construction and entrepreneurial practice.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Experimentation</h3><p>A digital fashion studio lets visitors test how width, weight and layers change movement.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Accessible choice</h3><p>German, English and French interfaces, sound controls and reduced-motion options support different ways of entering the experience.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-role">
        <p class="case-number">Role and contribution</p>
        <h2>Learning Experience Design · Concept · Research · Interaction Design · Visual Direction · Prototyping</h2>
        <p>This working prototype is still being refined. The current version demonstrates the complete learning journey and its central interactions; evaluation and documented learner feedback will be added as the project develops.</p>
      </section>

      <nav class="case-next" aria-label="Project navigation">
        <p>Next case study</p>
        <a href={`${base}projects/learning-differently`}>Learning, Differently <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </nav>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
